\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 02 \'b7 The Problem\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f2\i\b0\fs32 \cf0 Asset:
\f1\i0  02-1.webp\

\f2\i Caption:
\f1\i0  Starting point in the Classic app: All Tools\
New designers didn't start on Raspberry AI. They started on a call. A scheduled session with a Raspberry rep walking them through the platform.\
That call wasn't just teaching tools. It answered a bigger question: where does this fit into how I already work? Raspberry is built as separate tools, but a designer's work runs in workflows. Turning sketches into product images with multiple colourways crosses several tools in sequence. Without that context, the platform read as fragmented features with no obvious connection.\

\f2\i Asset:
\f1\i0  02-2.webp\

\f2\i Caption:
\f1\i0  Starting point when creating a new board\
Raspberry raised the cost themselves, calling onboarding a significant drain on their team's time. They were also building Boards, a canvas workspace that transformed how the app is used.\
Two problems, in that order:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Replace a human onboarding path\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Teach an interaction the first solution wasn't built for\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa398\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 03 \'b7 Constraints\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\fs32 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 One tutorial experience, two interaction paradigms, designed two months apart.
\f1\b0  Classic shipped first; Boards came later, so the second system had to extend an existing pattern.\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Tutorials had to cross tool boundaries.
\f1\b0  Raspberry scoped by workflow, not by feature, so no tutorial could live inside a single tool. Every step had to anchor to screens someone else designed.\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Two audiences starting from opposite places.
\f1\b0  New designers knew nothing. Existing ones knew Classic and had to learn Boards.\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 No access to product analytics while designing.
\f1\b0  I had no drop-off data, so no way to tell which step was losing people.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa398\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 04 \'b7 The Decisions\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa373\partightenfactor0

\fs37\fsmilli18667 \cf0 1 \'97 Teach the workflow, not the tool\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f2\i\b0\fs32 \cf0 Asset:
\f1\i0  04-1-1.webp\

\f2\i Caption:
\f1\i0  Guided Workflows modal in Classic\
The product is organized by tool but the work is organized by workflow. Per-tool tutorials were the cheaper answer, self-contained and easy to scope. A middle option existed too: per-tool tutorials and a map of how they connect. Workflow tutorials meant one tutorial running across several tools in sequence.\

\f2\i Asset:
\f1\i0  04-1-2.webp\

\f2\i Caption:
\f1\i0  On-Body Workflow in Classic\
I went with workflows because the objective was comprehension of fit.\
Per-tool tutorials teach mechanics but can't show where Raspberry sits in a designer's process. A map can label a seam but doesn't walk anyone across it. Only a sequence does that, which is what the onboarding call had been doing.\
The cost: a tutorial couldn\'92t belong to a single screen. Progress had to survive navigation across tools, and a designer who left mid-workflow needed a way back to where they'd left off.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa373\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs37\fsmilli18667 \cf0 2 \'97 Teach the canvas by walking through it, not by showing it\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs32 \cf0 By the time Boards came back into scope, the classic tutorials had shipped. The question now was whether the existing pattern could fit onto a canvas.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf0 Asset:
\f1\i0  04-2-1.webp\

\f2\i Caption:
\f1\i0  Option 1: example file for the Color Up Workflow\
The first option was creating example files. A complete workflow laid out on a board, sketch through to recoloured product images, with notes around the canvas highlighting features and tips. A designer could duplicate it and use it as a playground.\

\f2\i Asset:
\f1\i0  04-2-2.webp\

\f2\i Caption:
\f1\i0  Option 2: step-by-step tutorial for the Color Up Workflow\
The second was to bring the classic app\'92s event-based steps into Boards anyway, carrying over the notes from the first idea, walking a designer through the same sketch-to-recolour sequence while highlighting important tips.\
I decided on the second: an example file shows a finished workflow, not how to get there, and that was the whole objective.\
A designer looking at a completed board sees what Raspberry can produce, not where it fits in their process.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa398\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 05 \'b7 Craft & System\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa373\partightenfactor0

\fs37\fsmilli18667 \cf0 Components fit for the job\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f2\i\b0\fs32 \cf0 Asset:
\f1\i0  05-1-1.webp\

\f2\i Caption:
\f1\i0  Color Up Workflow in Classic with tooltips\

\f2\i Asset:
\f1\i0  05-1-2.webp\

\f2\i Caption:
\f1\i0  Color Up Workflow in Boards with notes\
Classic tutorials use tooltips; Boards needed something gentler for designers who already knew the tools but not the canvas. I kept the progress tracker and swapped tooltips for canvas notes. Less intrusive and ignorable by anyone who didn't want them. Notes were cut before release, so what shipped carries just the tracker and step sequence.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa373\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs37\fsmilli18667 \cf0 Four workflows, one pattern\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f2\i\b0\fs32 \cf0 Asset:
\f1\i0  05-2-1.webp\

\f2\i Caption:
\f1\i0  Croquis Workflow in Classic\

\f2\i Asset:
\f1\i0  05-2-2.webp\

\f2\i Caption:
\f1\i0  Bestseller Workflow in Classic\

\f2\i Asset:
\f1\i0  05-2-3.webp\

\f2\i Caption:
\f1\i0  On-Body Workflow in Boards\
Four workflows run on one pattern (entry point, tracker, step sequence), with each workflow just content dropped in, not a new design. A fifth is a content exercise, not a design one, which matters for whoever extends this later.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa373\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs37\fsmilli18667 \cf0 Interrupted, resumed, carried between tools\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f2\i\b0\fs32 \cf0 Asset:
\f1\i0  05-3.mov\

\f2\i Caption:
\f1\i0  Prototype of returning to a workflow and navigating between tools\
A designer who leaves mid-sequence returns where they left off. Progress carries across the workflow rather than resetting per tool.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa373\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs37\fsmilli18667 \cf0 Accessibility: Reduced motion & keyboard-reachable\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f2\i\b0\fs32 \cf0 Asset:
\f1\i0  05-4.webp\

\f2\i Caption:
\f1\i0  Default vs reduced motion indicator\
The pulsing indicator drops to a static circle under reduced motion. Tooltip controls stay keyboard-reachable, including dismissal.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa398\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 06 \'b7 Ship & Collaboration\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f2\i\b0\fs32 \cf0 Asset:
\f1\i0  06.webp\

\f2\i Caption:
\f1\i0  Example of annotations added alongside designs\
We ran handoff as working sessions rather than a document: I walked the Raspberry team through the prototypes for both classic and Boards, answered questions live, and left annotations where the prototype couldn't carry the detail. I'd built those prototypes in Claude Code, making a live walk through possible rather than a spec review.\
Everything shipped except the canvas notes. Engineers were still building Boards while adding guided workflows on a short timeline. They still aren't in the product as of writing, and I\'92m not sure if that's a decision or a backlog.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa398\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 07 \'b7 The Outcome\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs32 \cf0 Both tutorial systems are live. I asked Raspberry for usage after the fact. In the classic workspace, where the data exists, 
\f0\b 1,168 designers viewed the workflows modal over 90 days: 558 picked a workflow, 390 reached Generate, 382 generated, 204 downloaded \'97 17.5% end to end.
\f1\b0 \
The journey matters more than the total. Almost nobody who starts a workflow fails to finish, but half who reach the home page never start one: 
\f0\b the sequences hold, the entry point leaks.
\f1\b0 \
However, finishing a workflow isn\'92t the same as understanding where Raspberry fits, and this is classic only; nothing equivalent exists for Boards.\
Whether this reduced onboarding calls is still unanswered. Call volume isn't in the analytics.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa398\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 08 \'b7 Reflection\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs32 \cf0 I spent the design worrying about granularity: how finely to break each workflow into steps. I settled it by argument, with no data. The numbers suggest I was worrying about the wrong half. The loss is at the entry point, which I designed as a way in, not as a persuasion. I didn\'92t ask which part was most likely to fail, and the answer was the part I'd thought about least. If I picked it back up, that's where I'd start: testing whether showing a workflow's finished result up front gets more people to begin one.\
}